  "proofIdeas": {
    "703": "For both identities L_y^2 c=id and c^3=id, so L_y^6=id. Five inverse row applications recover multiplication. The inverse row squares all equal c, and reindexing that common square proves both transfers. Eight short superposition steps establish c^3=id for the longer law; the rest is direct cancellation.",
    "1518": "Finite squaring is bijective. If r*r=y, then D(y,x)=x*(r*x) and D(y,y)=r. Hence D(D(y,y),D(x,D(y,x)))=x. Both transfers use the same argument. No uniform multiplication term for r is claimed."
  },
